From: Kitsune <km2077@foxmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: add L2B slow-path diagnostics

936 makes the bridge RX slow path observable through two counters, but
not through the data that decides whether the PPE left an unbound L2B
entry: 3357 of 3358 frames ended in fdb_rx_no_slot and the counters alone
cannot say whether the descriptor index was 0xffff, pointed at a bound
entry, or pointed at a non-bridge entry.

The AN7581 PPE derives from the MediaTek PPE, whose bridge implementation
does not let software fabricate an L2 entry. Hardware creates and owns
the L2 flow entry, even for bridged frames, and reports the slot once the
flow reaches the offload rate; software only fills the egress (action)
fields of that entry. A software-built native L2B entry would have to
provide the learned l2b.key itself, which the driver never writes and
which 930 explicitly preserves from hardware. That is why this patch adds
diagnostics instead of a self-build commit path.

Capture the first 32 frames that the gate refuses, without allocation and
without touching the PPE: descriptor index, raw CPU reason, raw ib1, the
raw word at the native L2B ib2 offset, the parsed state/type and the gate
outcome (range, no entry, bind, type). Add a 32-entry histogram of the raw
CPU reason, incremented for every GDM RX descriptor, so a wrong reason
encoding is visible too. Expose both through a new debugfs file,
/sys/kernel/debug/ppe/l2b_dbg, next to the existing fdb_* counters.

Also print the PPE configuration once after the NPU attach and add the
same read-only registers to /sys/kernel/debug/ppe/config. In particular,
airoha_ppe_hw_init() never writes REG_PPE_UNBIND_AGE (0x238), while the
MediaTek PPE this block derives from programs MIN_PACKETS/DELTA there; the
printed value is the one left by the bootloader/firmware. Nothing in this
patch writes a PPE register, starts a commit, or changes the forwarding
decision; the frames still go to the software bridge exactly as in 936.

This is 937-net-airoha-add-l2b-slow-path-diagnostics.patch.

Signed-off-by: Kitsune <km2077@foxmail.com>
---
